-- Who has asked to be e-mailed about what.
--
-- Four tables, one shape: a row's mere presence is the subscription. There is no
-- "subscribed = 0" state to store — not wanting an e-mail is not wanting a row,
-- which is exactly what a DELETE leaves behind. This mirrors `project_roles`
-- (see Roles.sq): a join table keyed on the pair, written with INSERT OR IGNORE
-- and DELETE, so a double-click subscribes once and un-subscribing what was
-- never subscribed is not an error.
--
-- All four cascade on either parent's deletion, for `project_roles`' reason: a
-- subscription to a project, an issue, a forum or a post that no longer exists
-- is not a subscription anyone would miss, and leaving the row would point an
-- e-mail at a destination the sender can no longer explain. The user side
-- cascades too — a deleted account takes its subscriptions with it, the same way
-- it takes its tokens and its grants.
--
-- Nothing keys on email here. The address lives on `users` (see Users.sq), and
-- the recipient queries below reach it by join at send time — so a user who
-- fills in or clears their address later is reflected without touching a
-- subscription row.
--
-- ── The pairing, which is what makes four tables read as two ideas ──────────
--
-- The issue side has "tell me about new things in this container" (a project's
-- new issues) and "tell me about changes to this one thing" (an issue's
-- updates). LNL-63 adds exactly the same pair one feature over: a forum's new
-- posts, and one post's new comments. Nothing about the forum pair is a new
-- shape, which is why it is four near-identical tables rather than one generic
-- `subscriptions(user_id, target_type, target_id)`.
--
-- That generic table was written out and rejected. A polymorphic target cannot
-- carry a foreign key — SQLite cannot key a column whose destination depends on
-- another column — so all four cascades above would move into Kotlin, on four
-- delete paths, one of which would eventually be added without it. The failure
-- is silent: nobody notices an e-mail that is never sent about a post that no
-- longer exists, they notice it as the notifier quietly resolving nothing. The
-- same argument is made at greater length about the attachment owner columns in
-- Attachments.sq, and it comes out the same way here.
--
-- ── Visibility is deliberately NOT in the recipient queries ─────────────────
--
-- LNL-63 requires that somebody who loses sight of a project stops receiving its
-- forum e-mail. That rule is `AccessControl.canReadProject`'s — public project,
-- or a system administrator, or a member — and it is *not* spelled again in SQL
-- below, even though it could be. `ProjectAudience` turns the same rule into a
-- set, and `ForumNotificationService` intersects these rows with it at send
-- time. Two copies of a visibility rule, one of them in a language where nobody
-- will look for it, is how the two come to disagree.
--
-- ── The audience is everyone; the address is no longer filtered here (LNL-109) ─
--
-- These four queries used to end `AND u.email IS NOT NULL`, dropping any
-- subscriber without an address so the notifier only ever saw people it could
-- mail. LNL-109 gives every notification an in-app twin, and the in-app row must
-- reach a subscriber who has no address on file — the bell does not need a
-- mailbox. So the address filter is gone from SQL: these now return the whole
-- audience with a *nullable* `email`, and the split — write an in-app row for all,
-- send an e-mail only to those with an address — is made once, in the notifier
-- (see EmailNotifier.kt's `NotificationDispatcher`). What stays here is the one
-- narrowing that is about *who*, not *how they are reached*: not the actor.

-- Per-user, per-project: "e-mail me when a new issue is created in this project."
CREATE TABLE project_new_issue_subscriptions (
    user_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
    project_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (user_id, project_id),
    FOREIGN KEY (user_id) REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    FOREIGN KEY (project_id) REFERENCES projects(id) ON DELETE CASCADE
);

-- Per-user, per-issue: "e-mail me about any updates to this issue."
CREATE TABLE issue_update_subscriptions (
    user_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
    issue_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (user_id, issue_id),
    FOREIGN KEY (user_id) REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    FOREIGN KEY (issue_id) REFERENCES issues(id) ON DELETE CASCADE
);

-- Per-user, per-forum: "e-mail me when somebody posts in this forum."
--
-- The forum's twin of `project_new_issue_subscriptions`, keyed on the forum
-- rather than on the project: a project may have a dozen forums, and watching
-- one of them is a statement about that room rather than about the project.
-- Nothing is ever auto-subscribed here — see `forum_post_subscriptions` for the
-- one place a row is written without somebody pressing a button.
CREATE TABLE forum_new_post_subscriptions (
    user_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
    forum_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (user_id, forum_id),
    FOREIGN KEY (user_id) REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    FOREIGN KEY (forum_id) REFERENCES forums(id) ON DELETE CASCADE
);

-- Per-user, per-post: "e-mail me when somebody comments on this post."
--
-- `issue_update_subscriptions`' twin, and it inherits that table's one automatic
-- write: publishing a post subscribes its author, exactly as publishing an issue
-- subscribes theirs. See ForumPostRoutes' PUT, where the decision is argued.
CREATE TABLE forum_post_subscriptions (
    user_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
    post_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (user_id, post_id),
    FOREIGN KEY (user_id) REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    FOREIGN KEY (post_id) REFERENCES forum_posts(id) ON DELETE CASCADE
);

subscribeProjectNewIssues:
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO project_new_issue_subscriptions (user_id, project_id) VALUES (?, ?);

unsubscribeProjectNewIssues:
DELETE FROM project_new_issue_subscriptions WHERE user_id = ? AND project_id = ?;

isSubscribedProjectNewIssues:
SELECT EXISTS(
    SELECT 1 FROM project_new_issue_subscriptions WHERE user_id = ? AND project_id = ?
);

subscribeIssueUpdates:
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO issue_update_subscriptions (user_id, issue_id) VALUES (?, ?);

unsubscribeIssueUpdates:
DELETE FROM issue_update_subscriptions WHERE user_id = ? AND issue_id = ?;

isSubscribedIssueUpdates:
SELECT EXISTS(
    SELECT 1 FROM issue_update_subscriptions WHERE user_id = ? AND issue_id = ?
);

-- Everyone watching an issue, by name, for the issue detail's watcher list.
-- Names only — the same `display_name ?: provider_name` every other screen
-- shows, and pointedly not the email: who is watching is visible to anyone who
-- can see the issue, but where they'd be mailed is not. Ordered by name because
-- it is a list a human reads.
watchersForIssue:
SELECT u.id, COALESCE(u.display_name, u.provider_name) AS name
FROM issue_update_subscriptions s
JOIN users u ON u.id = s.user_id
WHERE s.issue_id = ?
ORDER BY name COLLATE NOCASE;

-- The audience for a project's new issue: every subscriber, minus the one person
-- who just created it. You are not notified about your own action — the
-- `user_id != ?` is that exclusion, and the second bind is the actor. `email` is
-- returned as-is and may be null: the notifier writes an in-app row for everyone
-- here and mails only those whose address is present. See this file's preamble
-- (LNL-109) and EmailNotifier.kt.
recipientsForProjectNewIssue:
SELECT u.id, u.email, COALESCE(u.display_name, u.provider_name) AS name
FROM project_new_issue_subscriptions s
JOIN users u ON u.id = s.user_id
WHERE s.project_id = ? AND u.id != ?;

-- The audience for an issue update. Same shape and same actor exclusion as above;
-- see it for why each clause is here.
recipientsForIssueUpdate:
SELECT u.id, u.email, COALESCE(u.display_name, u.provider_name) AS name
FROM issue_update_subscriptions s
JOIN users u ON u.id = s.user_id
WHERE s.issue_id = ? AND u.id != ?;

-- ── Forums (LNL-63) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- The four below are the two above, one feature over. Written out rather than
-- generalised for the reason at the top of this file, and kept in the same order
-- and the same shape so that the pair reads as one idea applied twice.

subscribeForumNewPosts:
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO forum_new_post_subscriptions (user_id, forum_id) VALUES (?, ?);

unsubscribeForumNewPosts:
DELETE FROM forum_new_post_subscriptions WHERE user_id = ? AND forum_id = ?;

isSubscribedForumNewPosts:
SELECT EXISTS(
    SELECT 1 FROM forum_new_post_subscriptions WHERE user_id = ? AND forum_id = ?
);

subscribeForumPost:
INSERT OR IGNORE INTO forum_post_subscriptions (user_id, post_id) VALUES (?, ?);

unsubscribeForumPost:
DELETE FROM forum_post_subscriptions WHERE user_id = ? AND post_id = ?;

isSubscribedForumPost:
SELECT EXISTS(
    SELECT 1 FROM forum_post_subscriptions WHERE user_id = ? AND post_id = ?
);

-- The audience for a forum's new post: every watcher, minus whoever just posted.
-- Same actor bind as `recipientsForProjectNewIssue`, and `email` nullable for the
-- same LNL-109 reason; see it.
--
-- Note what is NOT here: whether the watcher can still see the project. That
-- narrowing is applied by ForumNotificationService against `ProjectAudience`,
-- deliberately — see this file's preamble. It narrows the in-app row too, not just
-- the e-mail: somebody who can no longer see the project should get neither.
recipientsForForumNewPost:
SELECT u.id, u.email, COALESCE(u.display_name, u.provider_name) AS name
FROM forum_new_post_subscriptions s
JOIN users u ON u.id = s.user_id
WHERE s.forum_id = ? AND u.id != ?;

-- The audience for a new comment on a watched post. Same shape again.
recipientsForForumPost:
SELECT u.id, u.email, COALESCE(u.display_name, u.provider_name) AS name
FROM forum_post_subscriptions s
JOIN users u ON u.id = s.user_id
WHERE s.post_id = ? AND u.id != ?;
